  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "there was / there were",
      "title_form": "There was / There were (Había)",
      "title_target": "There was / There were",
      "description_md_form": "Usamos **there was** (singular) y **there were** (plural) para hablar de cosas que existían en el pasado. Es el pasado de *there is* y *there are*.\n\n- **There was** + sustantivo singular: `There was a lake.` (Había un lago.)\n- **There were** + sustantivo plural: `There were two forests.` (Había dos bosques.)\n\n**Importante:** En español decimos \"había\" para ambos, pero en inglés la forma cambia según el número. La estructura es: **There + was/were + lugar**.\n\nPara negar, usamos **there wasn't** (singular) y **there weren't** (plural): `There wasn't a desert.` (No había un desierto.)",
      "description_md_target": "We use **there was** (singular) and **there were** (plural) to talk about things that existed in the past. It is the past of *there is* and *there are*.\n\n- **There was** + singular noun: `There was a lake.`\n- **There were** + plural noun: `There were two forests.`\n\n**Important:** In Spanish, we say \"había\" for both, but in English the form changes depending on the number. The structure is: **There + was/were + place**.\n\nFor negatives, we use **there wasn't** (singular) and **there weren't** (plural): `There wasn't a desert.`",
      "grammar_structure": "There + was/were + (not) + noun + place",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "there_was_were",
        "headers": [
          "Forma",
          "Singular",
          "Plural"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Afirmativo",
            "There was a lake.",
            "There were two lakes."
          ],
          [
            "Negativo",
            "There wasn't a lake.",
            "There weren't two lakes."
          ],
          [
            "Pregunta",
            "Was there a lake?",
            "Were there two lakes?"
          ]
        ]
      },
